28.3  awk

TL;DR
  - awk reads text line by line and splits each line into fields: $1, $2… and
    $NF, the last one.
  - A program is pattern { action }: the action runs for each line that matches
    the pattern.
  - -F sets the field separator. By default, fields are separated by runs of
    spaces and tabs.

      df -h | awk '{print $5, $6}'
      awk -F: '$3 >= 1000 && $3 < 65534 {print $1}' /etc/passwd
      awk '{sum += $2} END {print sum}' sales.txt

Pitfalls
  - Put the program in single quotes: inside double quotes, the shell replaces
    $1 before awk sees it.
  - -F, doesn't understand CSV: a comma inside quotes still splits the field.
    Use a real CSV tool for CSV.
  - print $1 $2 glues the fields together; print $1, $2 puts a space between
    them.
  - Debian and Ubuntu install mawk as awk, others gawk: in scripts, stay with
    the features both have.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with atfq 28.3 --why

